## 1. Introduction
The fluoride substance in drinking water has been reflected on the worldwide ecological troubles and has received wide consideration for the past decades. The extreme fluoride intake from water leads to fluorosis. There are more than twenty developed and
developing countries were affected by fluorosis [1]. Hence, the WHO fixed the highest permitted fluoride content in potable water as \(1.5~\mathrm{mg/L}\) [2]. Despite growing worries about the excess fluoride threat, developing effective defluoridation methods remains a pressing challenge. Up to now, the numerous drinking water purification methods containing adsorption, reverse osmosis, precipitation, anion exchange, electrocoagulation and membrane process [3-7] were promisingly used for fluoride retention. Among these techniques adsorption method is found to be a capable and economical approach due to its numerous benefits, like high efficiency, low energy/cost, and operational flexibility [8]. Adsorption materials like activated alumina, carbon-based materials, iron-based sorbent materials, chitosan, montmorillonite, calcium-based material, metal oxides, layered double hydroxides, cellulose, kaolinite and metal oxides [9-15] have been widely used for removal of fluoride. But these adsorption materials possess some limitations like poor selectivity and adsorption capacity [16]. Though, the advanced and high-performance adsorption materials that is capable of revealing rapid and greater adsorption capacity for fluoride ions is yet to be investigated. Here, hydroxyapatite (HAp) based hybrid material like HAp layered metal organic frameworks (MOFs) based materials were fabricated for fluoride removal.
HAp \(\left[\mathsf{C a}_{10}(\mathsf{P O}_{4})_{6}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}\right]\) act as a potential adsorbent material for ecological route since it has a high affinity and interaction for the adsorption of numerous pollutants of ecological attention [17]. The characteristics of HAp was well recognized and their mechanisms of retention depend upon the chemical species. HAp possesses unique properties like a crystalline solid, hexagonal system, higher stability potential, and ion exchanger [18]. Besides, HAp could act as a host to small molecules. However, HAp reveals very low water solubility under basic medium and low cost material [19].
HAp has been investigated to be most potential candidate for defluoridation of water as a result of its high recyclability, good biocompatibility and easy synthesis [20]. Sundaram et al. investigated that adsorption capacity of HAp and chitosan/HAp adsorbents towards fluoride removal possess 4.7 and \(1.56~\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) respectively [21]. Here two main features leading towards the fluoride adsorption on HAp via electrostatic interaction and ion exchange mechanism. Tang et al reported that fluorinated HAp and/or fluorapatite are thermodynamically high stable are formed easily [22]. The field application of HAp towards defluoridation from potable water is still restricted by its normal fluoride adsorption capacity and it has been soundly recognized that material with larger surface areas and more active/binding sites could be developed through altering the micro-pore size and surface morphology [23]. Besides, materials with hierarchically structure meso/macroporous frameworks allow the rapid diffusion of the guest species in the host channel material, as a result assists their access to binding sites [24]. Amongst different kinds of adsorbent materials, metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) are predominantly smart and attractive because they not only have the characteristics stated over, however also can perform as good adsorbent material used for defluoridation.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are organic–inorganic crystalline porous hybrid material consists of positively charged metal species surrounded with linker (organic molecules) with cage-like structure having larger surface area [25]. Besides, the MOFs have the unique properties like tunable pore size, network topology, geometry, chemical functionality, crystalline porous materials, dimension and uniform pore structures [26]. These unique properties help researchers in the successful control of porosity, framework topology and functionality. MOFs are effective material for potential applications like catalysis, adsorption, fuel cells, batteries, supercapacitors, imaging and drug delivery [27-32]. Fluoride spe
cies act as a hard base having strong binding affinity towards polyvalent metal ions/species due to its higher electro-negativity nature and small ionic size [33]. In the interim, rare earth metal, like lanthanum is considered as a hard acid, reveal a strong attraction towards fluoride species [34].
Recently, Tan et al have studied zirconium-MOFs towards fluoride removal [35]. Jeyaseelan et al have reported graphene based alginate beads for fluoride removal [36]. Zhao et al have reported Ce- MOFs for fluoride adsorption [37]. Guiza et al have prepared clay based material for defluoridation [38]. Zhu et al reported zirconium fumarate (MOF-801) for toxic anions adsorption [39]. Ke et al investigated fumarate based MOFs for fluoride removal [40]. Gao et al have focused the zirconia/zeolite MOFs material for fluoride adsorption [41]. Only limited number of reports have been potentially utilized for toxic ions/fluoride adsorption from water by MOFs based hybrid materials. Hence, in this study combination of HAp with La-based MOFs were developed by layer by layer (LBL) method for fluoride removal from water. The directed template synthesis method is very easy, uncomplicated, and well developed way for in situ nucleation and growth of nanocrystals. The coulomb and chelation attraction between inbuilt \({\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{a}}^{2+}\) ions on HAp surface, \({\mathrm{La}}^{3+}\) and linker (COO −) of La-MOFs would stimulate the initial nucleation followed by growth of material by avoiding the complicate surface alteration [42]. Besides, the LBL technique permits standardized deposition and accurate thickness, therefore potentially utilized in fluoride removal. In addition, HAp and La-MOFs have good biocompatibility.
In this present topic, the developed HAp-La-BTC MOFs are promisingly employed for defluoridation studies. In addition, batch adsorption studies were performed to prove the fluoride adsorption nature and adsorption isotherm/kinetic behavior of HAp-LaBTC MOFs. The regeneration and field application studies of the HAp-La based MOFs were explored. The sophisticated instrument techniques like FTIR, XRD, TEM, SEM, TGA and EDAX investigations was studied to recognize the character of adsorbent and properties of HAp-La based MOFs with its fluoride adsorption. Finally, mechanism of fluoride adsorption on HAp-La based MOFs was also elucidated.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid (BTC), lanthanum chloride heptahydrate, sodium nitrate, sodium hydroxide, sodium chloride, acetic acid, sodium bicarbonate, sodium fluoride, ethanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\mathrm{OH}\right)\), anhydrous potassium dihydrogen phosphate, oleic acid, sodium sulphate and calcium chloride \(\left(\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}\right)\) were procured from Merck, India.
## 2.2. Preparation of adsorbents
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of HAp
Firstly, the mixture of \(\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}\) (0.22 g in 20 mL), \(\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (0.28 g in 10 mL) and NaOH (1 g in 20 mL) were developed and added drop by drop into oleic acid (10 g) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}\) (14 g) mixture under stirring. The developed mixture then poured into hydrothermal autoclave (Telfon-lined coated) at 180 °C for 24 h. Finally, the well-structured HAp nano-rods material were completely washed several times with ethanol and deionized water, further cooled to ambient atmosphere and stored in absolute ethyl alcohol for further modification.
## 2.2.2. Preparation of HAp-La based MOFs
2.2.2. Preparation of HAp-La based MOFsThe synthesis of HAp-La-BTC MOFs were carried out using the template-directed LBL technique [42]. In short, the HAp (100 ~ 200 mg) containing ethanol dispersions were centrifuged for ethanol removal. Then, add 10 mL of BTC ethanol solution (10 mmol L\(^{-1}\)), then the content mixture was heated at 60 °C for 30 min. The resultant material was dipped in ethanol (10 mL) containing lanthanum chloride heptahydrate (LaCl\(_3\)\(\cdot\)7H\(_2\)O) with a concentration of 10 mmol L\(^{-1}\) at 60 °C for 15 min, which was centrifuged and followed by washed with ethanol. The schematic diagram of HAp-La-BTC MOFs was shown in Fig. 1.
## 2.3. Defluoridation experiments
2.3. Defluoridation experimentsThe prepared HAp-La based MOFs employed for the removal of fluoride from aquatic system and it was investigated by batch studies with room temperature. Acid/base condition of HAp-La-BTC MOFs with fluoride adjusted using dil. NaOH and HCl. In the iodine flask, about 50 mL of fluoride solution (10 mg/L) with 0.1 g of HAp-La based MOFs was taken. Afterward, the iodine flask contents were shaken with adsorption time at room temperature (200 rpm speed) and the final fluoride concentration was measured. The kinetics and isotherms exploration was executed under various fluoride concentration solutions (8, 10, 12 and 14 mg/L) with altered temperatures (303, 313 and 323 K). The DC of HAp-La-BTC MOFs was calculated using our previous reports [43].
## 2.4. Analysis
2.4. AnalysisThe fluoride and pH concentration was studied with fluoride ion selective electrode and pH electrode (Thermo Orion model: VERSA STAR92). The pH\(_{zpc}\) of HAp-La-BTC MOFs was analyzed using pH drift technique.
## 2.5. Characterization studies
2.5. Characterization studiesThe presence of functional groups of HAp-La-BTC MOFs was analyzed by FTIR (Thermo Nicolet 380). The crystalline nature and surface morphology of HAp-La-BTC MOFs by HR-TEM (JEOL-2100+). FE-SEM [Quanta FEG 250] was employed to examine the morphological nature of HAp-La-BTC MOFs. For determination of thermal stability of HAp-La-BTC MOFs, TGA (Universal V4.5A TA) was employed. The presence of elements in the HAp-La based MOFs was determined with EDAX (Bruker Nano GMBH).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization investigations
## 3.1.1. FTIR investigation
3.1.1. FTIR investigationFTIR result of HAp shows the most crucial bands at 1630 and 3419 cm\(^{-1}\) owing to H\(_2\)O groups, while the strong stretching vibrations and deformation of OH\(^-\) were noted at 3418 and 649 cm\(^{-1}\) respectively [44]. Next, the stretching vibration of PO\(_3^2\)\(^-\) was noted between 900 and 1100 cm\(^{-1}\) while their intense sharp peaks in the form of bending mode was detected at 520 and 567 cm\(^{-1}\) [45]. In addition, the P\(\rightarrow\)O stretching in PO\(_3^2\)\(^-\) was noted at 1014 and 961 cm\(^{-1}\) and their weak bending vibration was noted at 463 cm\(^{-1}\). Moreover, the significant band HPO\(_4^2\)\(^-\) groups were appeared at 874 cm\(^{-1}\) [46]. The resultant CO\(_5\)\(^-\) band detected at 1402-1460 cm\(^{-1}\), which may due to absorption of atmospheric CO\(_2\) during preparation of sample. The characteristic -OH vibrations in HAp has attributed as weak band at 3642 cm\(^{-1}\) which ensures it good formation [47] (c.f. Fig. 2a).The symmetric and asymmetric stretching FTIR bands of -OCO\(^-\) was noticed at 1374 and 1645 cm\(^{-1}\); C=C bands of BTC ligand was detected at 1586 and 1452 cm\(^{-1}\) [48] (c.f. Fig. 2b). La-O stretching was observed close to 700 and 400 cm\(^{-1}\). The -OH group band of HAp-La based MOFs were detected at 3415 cm\(^{-1}\). Primarily, the- COO\(^-\) band in COOH of HAp-La based MOFs has the strong and sharp stretching vibration at 1577 cm\(^{-1}\). The remarkable band of La-OH was appeared as strong vibration band (1104 cm\(^{-1}\)) exposes the coordination bond (COO\(^-\)…\(\cdot\cdot\cdot\cdot\) La\(^{3+}\)) [49]. Also, the benzene ring band was noticed at 925 and 762 cm\(^{-1}\). By comparing FTIR spectra of HAp, a new absorption band come out for HAp-La-BTC MOFs at 1700 cm\(^{-1}\) which corresponds to the C=O bond stretching vibration, and the C=C (phenyl ring) stretching absorption bands at 1627, 1450 and 1384 cm\(^{-1}\) [50].FTIR bands of HAp-La based MOFs were else come back into fluoride treated HAp-La based MOFs which may confirm that HAp-La-BTC MOFs bands significantly present in the fluoride treated HAp-La based MOFs. The major benzene ring band of BTC was a little shrunken in the fluoride treated HAp-La based MOFs and the band of La\(^{3+}\) (1104 cm\(^{-1}\)) was sturdily shortened in the fluoride treated HAp-La based MOFs which may terminate the efficient fluoride adsorption [51]. The electrostatic attraction mechanism which is occurred between La\(^{3+}\) and F\(^-\) might be the strong rationale for the intensity reduced in FTIR bands (c.f. Fig. 2b). The corresponding -OH groups intensity of fluoride adsorbed HAp-La based MOFs was modified also which is due to the ion-exchange mechanism happened between fluoride ions and OH\(^-\) ions of HAp-La based MOFs.
The symmetric and asymmetric stretching FTIR bands of –OCO– was noticed at 1374 and \(1645~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ; \(\scriptstyle{C=C}\) bands of BTC ligand was detected at 1586 and \(1452~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [48] (c.f. Fig. 2b). La-O stretching was observed close to 700 and \(400~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) . The –OH group band of HAp-La based MOFs were detected at \(3415~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) . Primarily, the– COO\(^-\) band in COOH of HAp-La based MOFs has the strong and sharp stretching vibration at \(1577\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) . The remarkable band of La-OH was appeared as strong vibration band \((1104\ \ c m^{-1})\) ) exposes the coordination bond \((\mathsf{C O O}^{-}....\ldots\mathsf{L a}^{3+})\) [49]. Also, the benzene ring band was noticed at 925 and \(762~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) . By comparing FTIR spectra of HAp, a new absorption band come out for HAp-La-BTC MOFs at \(1700\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) which corresponds to the \(\scriptstyle{C=C}\) bond stretching vibration, and the \(\scriptstyle{C=C}\) (phenyl ring) stretching absorption bands at 1627, 1450 and \(1384~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [50].
FTIR bands of HAp-La based MOFs were else come back into fluoride treated HAp-La based MOFs which may confirm that HAp-LaBTC MOFs bands significantly present in the fluoride treated HAPla based MOFs. The major benzene ring band of BTC was a little shrunken in the fluoride treated HAp-La based MOFs and the band of \(\mathrm{La}^{3+}\) ( \(1104~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ) was sturdily shortened in the fluoride treated HAp-La based MOFs which may terminate the efficient fluoride adsorption [51]. The electrostatic attraction mechanism which is occurred between \(\mathrm{La}^{3+}\) and F\(^-\) might be the strong rationale for the intensity reduced in FTIR bands (c.f. Fig. 2b). The corresponding – OH groups intensity of fluoride adsorbed HAp-La based MOFs was modified also which is due to the ion-exchange mechanism happened between fluoride ions and OH\(^-\) ions of HAp-La based MOFs.
## 3.1.2. XRD analysis
3.1.2. XRD analysisXRD investigation of HAp and HAp-La based MOFs were portrayed in Fig. 3a. The Bragg peaks with miller indices (000), (102), (210), (300), (202), (130), (131), (222), (213) (004) and (304) were linked with HAp. The HAp sample with raised intensity and reduced HAp-La-BTC MOFs which indicates crystallinity reduced after incorporation of HAp [52]. The XRD diffraction peak of HAp and HAp-La-BTC MOFs could be depicted in Fig. 3a, which could be portrayed to a single phase of HAp (JCPDS File No. 74-0566). Besides to the XRD spectra of HAp, one or more diffraction peaks are present in the XRD peak of HAp-La-BTC MOFs nanomaterial originated from La-BTC MOFs crystalline phase indicates the developed nanomaterial were collection of both HAp and La-BTC MOFs.
## 3.1.3. TGA investigations
3.1.3. TGA investigationsTGA and DTA study of HAp-La based MOFs were shown in Fig. 3b and these results exhibits that the major three stages of weight losses. The first stage of weight loss of HAp-La-BTC MOFs were attributed to \(1.3\ \%\) that was mainly observed in 10 to \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)
range was corresponds to the presence of OH groups in the developed HAp-La based MOFs. The next weight loss was observed as \(3.9\ \%\) and this was noticed at \(80{-}260\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) due to the entrapped chemically adsorbed water particles, therefore amorphous was distorted to the crystalline hexagonal phase. Then, next weight loss was observed at \(350{-}500\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) range as \(0.8\ \%\) due to the breakdown of \(\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}\) and other impurities [53]. Though, the obtained DTA result of HAp-La based MOFs indicates high thermal stability. The DTA results of HAp-La based MOFs shows the exothermic peaks (200 to \(310\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)).
## 3.1.4. TEM and SEM investigations
3.1.4. TEM and SEM investigationsThe morphological surface of the HAp-La based MOFs revealed that rod-like porous structure along with uneven size at \(5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) (Fig. 4a). After defluoridation, the morphological surface of the prepared HAp-La based MOFs acquires modified because of fluoride adsorption in the HAp-La-BTC MOFs pores (Fig. 4b). In addition, TEM results also exhibit that the HAp-La-BTC MOFs have rod-like structure at 500 and \(200\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 4c and d).
## 3.1.5. EDAX studies
The vital elemental (Ca, O, C, P and La) peaks of HAp-La based MOFs show the excellent development of HAp-La based MOFs (c. f. Fig. 5a). After defluoridation, the matching atomic elements namely Ca, C, O, P and La peaks with occurrence of the fluoride elemental peak clearly designates the fluoride has successfully adsorbed on the surface of HAp-La-BTC MOFs (c.f. Fig. 5b).
## 3.2. Impact on shaking time
The manipulate of shaking time acting a imperative function in the perceptive of reactive system of fluoride species and the equilibrium time which is sturdly depends on aspects like material particle size or surface area, pore structure and the result achieved for fluoride adsorption as utility of contact time for HAp-La based MOFs was portrayed in Fig. 6a. All the other factors like shaking speed with \(200\;\mathrm{rpm}\), adsorbent dosage \((0.1\mathrm{g})\), initial concentration of fluoride \((10\;\mathrm{mg/L})\) and pH were kept as constant. The prepared HAp and HAp-La-BTC MOFs have the greatest DCs of 1298 and \(4255\;\mathrm{mgF^{-}\;kg^{-1}}\) within 40 and \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) respectively. The contact potential between fluoride and HAp-La-BTC MOFs binding sites in aqueous solution would be higher due to the strong adsorption property of the developed HAp-La-BTC MOFs. Hence, the further adsorption experiments were focused only for HAp-La based MOFs by \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) saturation time.
## 3.3. Impact of dosage
The role of HAp-La based MOFs dosage (0.05 to \(0.25\;\mathrm{g}\)) towards defluoridation was studied under \(10\;\mathrm{mg/L}\) fluoride solution. It was mainly observed that DC was improved at first between 0.05 and
\(0.1\;\mathrm{g}\) of HAp-La based MOFs dosage and above \(0.1\;\mathrm{g}\) DC does not get much improved. This may be owing to the amount and accessibility for the reactive sites of HAp-La based MOFs possibly greater at higher dosage ranges than for occupying fluoride species onto its surface (c.f. Fig. 6b). The HAp-La based MOFs attains fluoride equilibrium at \(0.1\;\mathrm{g}\) and therefore, \(0.1\;\mathrm{g}\) of HAp-La based MOFs dosage was preferred as optimized dosage level for the following defluoridation investigations.
## 3.4. Impact of fluoride adsorbate concentration
The influence of initial fluoride concentration was investigated via altering their concentrations from 2 to \(14\;\mathrm{mg/L}\) which results are shown in Fig. 6c. The observed results were exhibit that DC was enhanced between 2 and \(10\;\mathrm{mg/L}\) with increase of initial fluoride concentration. After \(10\;\mathrm{mg/L}\), the DC does not get raised owing to the material has reached equilibrium. The improved fluoride concentration recommended that the driving forces which conquer the mass transfer resistant at surface of HAp-La based MOFs and fluoride upto \(10\;\mathrm{mg/L}\). Accordingly, about \(10\;\mathrm{mg/L}\) initial concentration was fixed as the optimum concentration of fluoride for further studies.
## 3.5. Impact of solution pH
It is important to explore the role of HAp-La based MOFs on the adsorption of fluoride with assorted pH situations. The pH experiments were studied in the pH ranges of 3 to 12 (c.f. Fig. 6d). The main observable factor is that the pH of the solution was improved between 3 and 6 which also increase the DC. On the other hand, the DC reduced about \(\mathrm{pH}>6\) [54]. HAp-La based MOFs contain the
optimal DC at \(\mathsf{p H}\,6\) . The consequence \(\mathsf{p H}_{\mathsf{z p c}}\) rate of HAp-La-BTC MOFs was noted as 5.08 (c.f. Fig. 6c). At basic circumstances, the \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) ions will compete with fluoride on the active sites of HApLa-BTC MOFs and hence the fluoride adsorption was diminished. Besides, the final \(\mathsf{p H}\) of fluoride adsorbed solution was measured to be neutral exhibits the HAp-La based MOFs may be employed at diverse pH conditions.
## 3.6. Impact of co-existing anions
In this co-existing anions study, numerous types of characteristic co-existing anions like \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\) , \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\,\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\,\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\) ions effect on the adsorption process were examined by adding \(200~\mathrm{{\mg/L}}\) of \(\mathrm{NaHCO_{3}}\) , \(\mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\) , \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) , NaCl and \(\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}\) to \(10~\mathrm{mg/L}\) fluoride concentration and the results are given in Fig. 6f. The various anions have different effect on the fluoride adsorption because some anions would enhance columbic forces and some would compete with fluoride for the active sites of adsorbent. Fig. 6g indicates that DC of HAp-La-BTC MOFs gets slightly decreased from \(4465~\mathrm{mgF}^{-}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) to 4320, 4180 and \(4240\;\mathrm{mgF}^{-}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) in the presence of chloride, sulphate and nitrate respectively which indicates negligible effects on fluoride removal. In addition, the adsorption capacities of HAp-La based MOFs decreased to 3000 and \(3800~\mathrm{mgF^{-}~k g^{-1}}\) in the presence of \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) respectively and have significant influence on fluoride removal. The decreased DC may be ascribed to the rivalry between the fluoride ions, \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) for the active sites of HAp-La-BTC MOFs. For fluoride adsorption the impacts of \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\) have the two vital characteristics, first one is the similar ionic size of \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\) with fluoride and the second one is the hydrolysis of \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\) which can give \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) ion which influence fluoride adsorption.
## 3.7. Ion selectivity study
Selectivity studies of HAp-La based MOFs on fluoride adsorption along with other co-existing anions viz., \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\) , \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\,\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}\) , \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\,\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\)
and \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\) were studied and these results are given in Fig. 6g. The results reveal that the HAp-La-BTC MOFs appreciably removes fluoride in the presence of other co-ions \(\mathrm{(HCO_{3}^{-}}\) , \(\mathrm{HCrO_{4}}\) , \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\,\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\,\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\) ). From Fig. 6g results shows that HAp-La-BTC MOFs possess higher DC \((4255~\mathrm{mgF^{-}~k g^{-1}})\) gets decreases in the presence of other adsorbate solutions viz., \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\) , \(\mathrm{HCrO_{4}^{-}}\) , \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\,\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\,\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\) having FCS of 3020, 3150, 3855, 4050, 3900 and \(3950~\mathrm{mgF^{-}~k g^{-1}}\) respectively which indicates the selectivity nature of HAp-La based MOFs towards fluoride in the presence of other co-ions.
## 3.8. Isotherm studies
The classic adsorption isotherms viz., Dubinin-Radushkevich (D–R), Freundlich and Langmuir models are investigated to portray the fluoride adsorption onto HAp-La-BTC MOFs results were shown in Table 1. The detailed fitting parameters of Freundlich [55], Langmuir [56] and Dubinin-Radushkevich (D–R) [57] models were given in Table 1. Furthermore, the defluoridation mechanism could be investigated by the D-R model free energy (E). In general, the E value range \(<8\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) demonstrated a physical adsorption route, while defluoridation was governed by electrostatic interaction \(\mathrm{E}>8\ \ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) condition, chemical adsorption was dominated [58]. According to the parameter results, the E value ranged nearly \(12\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) demonstrating the defluoridation was mainly governed by chemisorption via electrostatic interaction.
## 3.9. Thermodynamics studies
HAp-La based MOFs have the positively charged group's favors for accessibility and reactivity of the fluoride ions by enrichment. In addition, reaction (pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order) [60,61] and diffusion (particle and intraparticle diffusion) based models were employed [62,63] and the obtained results were illustrated in Table S1. The consequent pore diffusion may be related to the hierarchically porous structure of HAp-La based MOFs on fluoride investigated by diffusion models. In addition, the intraparticle diffusion model helps to explain the diffusion route of fluoride ions, and these model parameter results were shown in Table S1. As seen in Table S1, the fluoride removal process is well described by the intraparticle and pseudo-second-order model with high \( r \) \((r>0.992)\) values signifying the strong chemical interaction.
## 3.11. Defluoridation mechanism
The prepared HAp-La-BTC MOFs possess \(\mathrm{La}^{3+}\) (hard acid) and \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) from HAp which leads to the adsorption of fluoride ion by means of complexation and electrostatic attraction [64] (c.f. Fig. 7). The corresponding –OH groups intensity of fluoride adsorbed HAp-La-BTC MOFs were altered and this phenomenon gives rise to competitive adsorption that might be considered as ion exchange mechanism [65]. However, the fluoride removal mechanism of HAp-La-BTC MOFs briefly explained in FTIR and EDAX studies under sections 3.1.1 and 3.1.5.
## 3.12. Adsorption capacity evaluation study
Table 3 results majorly exhibited that HAp-La-BTC MOFs possesses the maximum fluoride adsorption capacity compared with many other adsorbents reported in the literature. The prepared HAp-La-BTC MOFs acquired the highest fluoride adsorption capacity of \(4255~\mathrm{mgF^{-}\,k g^{-1}}\) \(\left(30\,\mathrm{min}\right)\) . Therefore, it was majorly detected that the HAp-La based MOFs possess highest DC with 30 min minimum saturation time. The highest DC of HAp-La based MOFs was mainly due to the presence of active functional groups and La-metal ions in their structure.
## 3.13. Field suitability
The fittingness of HAp-La based MOFs at field conditions was determined by actual field water sample collected from nearby village in Dindigul district of Tamilnadu. Briefly, actual field investigation was performed with \(0.1\textbf{g}\) of HAp-La based MOFs in field water \(\left(50\;\mathrm{mL}\right)\) , and then it was shaken with optimal reaction time, further fluoride concentration was observed (c.f. Table 4). At first,
the initial fluoride concentration was noticed as \(3.14~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}\) and the field sample treated with HAp-La based MOFs possess the final fluoride concentration was observed as \(1.12~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}\). The reduction in the fluoride concentration shows the field applicability of the material. Additionally, HAp-La-BTC MOFs also controlled the other water quality parameters and these results are depicted in Table 4.
## 3.14. Reutilize studies
The regeneration ability of HAp-La-BTC MOFs was executed with \(0.1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{NaOH}\) as promising eluent [86]. As depicted in Fig. 8, the adsorption efficiency of HAp-La based MOFs was diminished upto \(20\%\) after regeneration of six cycles indicates HAp-La-BTC MOFs was recycled successfully upto six times.
## 4. Conclusions
The HAp-La-based MOFs were demonstrated to be efficient adsorbent material for the fluoride retention from water at batch conditions. The highest DC of HAp-La-BTC MOFs was identified to be \(4255\;\mathrm{mgF}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at \(30\,\mathrm{min}\). At thermodynamic study reveals that the \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) negative values at three temperatures designated the spontaneous nature of defluoridation, while \(\Delta S^{\circ}\) and \(\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}\) positive values demonstrating the adsorption of fluoride onto HAp-La-BTC MOFs is an endothermic reaction and random state/high affinity toward target fluoride ions. In kinetics studies, the fluoride removal process is well described by the intraparticle and pseudo-second-order models with high r values signifying the strong chemical interaction. The regeneration and field study results exhibited that the developed material was regenerable and applicable at field level. The obtained results demonstrated that the developed HAp-La-BTC MOFs utilized for promising fluoride removal.
